  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "Prove that if $c$ is an odd integer, then the equation $x^2+x+c=0$ has no integer solution.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "No integer solution exists: integer roots would both be odd, but their sum is $-1$, which is odd.",
      "worked": "Suppose there is an integer root $p$. The other root is $q=-1-p$, so $q$ is also an integer. Hence\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+x+c&=(x-p)(x-q)\\\\x^2+x+c&=x^2-(p+q)x+pq.\\end{align*}$$\nComparing coefficients gives $pq=c$ and $p+q=-1$. Since $c$ is odd, both $p$ and $q$ are odd. Their sum is therefore even, contradicting $p+q=-1$. Thus no integer solution exists.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 60
  },
